lls. Concerns: nasty remarks (crew cooperation, professional communication), weak memory for numbers (attention/accuracy). Overall maybe okay but communication issue serious.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Strengths: decision quality, calm under pressure, technical competence. Concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Could be problematic for crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, has hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, concentration, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: know-it-all (cooperation), hot temper (stress resilience). Mixed.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Likely Candidate B has strong reliability and stress handling but communication issue is severe. Candidate C has good decision and calm but communication and egocentric. Candidate D has responsibility but hot temper. Candidate A has leadership but not open to ideas and show-off.\n\nWhich is best? Perhaps Candidate B's conscientiousness and stress handling are crucial, but nasty remarks could be fatal. Might be mitigated? But crew cooperation is essential. Candidate C's calm and decision making are strong, but egocentric and communication issues also problematic. Candidate D's hot temper is concerning for stress resilience. Candidate A's not open to new ideas may affect adaptability.\n\nMaybe Candidate B is still strongest overall despite communication issue, because reliability and stress handling are top. But we need to argue.\n\nWe can start by recommending Candidate B, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns, and suggest we need to discuss communication further.\n\nLet's produce public message: \"I recommend Candidate B...